  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The query is a neutral, highly lipophilic N-formylleucine ester of a hexyl-substituted beta-lactone with a long alkyl chain (logP 6.88, MW 495.75, TPSA 81.7, 23 rotatable bonds), placing it in the same poorly soluble lipophilic ester/lactone class as testosterone undecanoate; direct human absolute F for that class is low.",
      "card_ids": ["C15", "C30", "C13", "C16"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Direct human absolute F for lipophilic long-chain ester prodrugs is below threshold: testosterone undecanoate 6.8 ± 3.3% (C15) and 6.83 ± 3.32% vs IV (C30), oral testosterone 3.66 ± 2.45% (C13), and crystalline testosterone undecanoate poor (C16).",
      "card_ids": ["C15", "C30", "C13", "C16"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Lipophilic neutral poorly soluble drugs with extensive first-pass metabolism have absolute oral F below 20%: felodipine 12.5–15.4% in fasted solution/tablet studies (C01, C04, C11, C25) and nimodipine 3.5–3.8% across 30–90 mg fasted doses (C03, C09, C21, C28).",
      "card_ids": ["C01", "C04", "C11", "C25", "C03", "C09", "C21", "C28"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Batimastat, a poorly soluble peptidomimetic with amide/hydroxamate functionality, showed low/poor oral bioavailability in patients (C05, C06, C08, C27), with L2 context describing it as nonorally bioavailable (C32) and poor (C36); this supports low F for the similarly poorly soluble peptide-ester-like query.",
      "card_ids": ["C05", "C06", "C08", "C27", "C32", "C36"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "C75 shows simvastatin lactone is hydrolyzed by nonspecific carboxyesterases in the intestinal wall, liver, and plasma; this is mechanistically consistent with presystemic loss for a lactone/ester, but it is not a direct F measurement for the query and does not establish the outcome.",
      "card_ids": ["C75"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic clearance/metabolic stability records show that lipophilic esters and related compounds undergo substantial hepatic metabolism/glucuronidation (e.g., DEHP hydrolysis HLM Clint 59.7, MEHP glucuronidation 68.3/53.5, 10-gingerol glucuronide 68.13, HLM Clint 19.2–147.7), mechanistically supporting presystemic loss for the query's ester/lactone; however these are not query-specific or direct F measurements.",
      "card_ids": ["C77", "C79", "C81", "C82", "C88", "C87", "C78", "C86"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "low",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C77", "C78", "C79", "C81", "C82", "C83", "C85", "C86", "C87", "C88"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability measurement for the query molecule itself is supplied; the decision relies on transfer from structurally analogous lipophilic esters and poorly soluble neutral drugs.",
    "No matched oral-versus-IV exposure, dose, formulation, or food-effect data are available for the query.",
    "No query-specific hepatic clearance or metabolic stability measurement is available; L6 records are for other molecules and mostly in vitro/animal systems.",
    "No query-specific P-gp, PEPT, esterase, or CYP data are available.",
    "The extent of intestinal/liver hydrolysis of the query's ester and beta-lactone, and its first-pass metabolism, are not directly quantified.",
    "C38 notes lipid co-administration can increase lipophilic ester bioavailability via lymphatic uptake, but this is relative and not query-specific; it does not establish F >= 20%."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted low (F < 20%) because the most transferable direct human F evidence—lipophilic long-chain ester testosterone undecanoate (~6.8%), poorly soluble neutral drugs felodipine (12.5–15.4%) and nimodipine (3.5–3.8%), and poorly soluble peptidomimetic batimastat (low/poor)—all fall below threshold; L6 hepatic clearance/metabolic stability records for lipophilic esters and related compounds mechanistically support presystemic loss but are not query-specific and do not change the prediction."
}

